WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:08.130
Welcome back. In this week, we'll learn to make linear regression much faster and much more powerful.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:13.130
And by the end of this week, you'll be two thirds of the way to finishing this first course.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:21.130
Let's start by looking at a version of linear regression that can look at not just one feature, but a lot of different features.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:22.130
Let's take a look.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:31.130
In the original version of linear regression, you had a single feature x, the size of the hulls,

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:35.130
and you're able to predict y, the price of the hulls.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:42.130
So the model was f wb of x equals wx plus b.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:49.130
But now what if you did not only have the size of the hulls as a feature with which to try to predict the price.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:56.130
But if you also knew the number of bedrooms, the number of floors, and the age of the home in years,

00:00:56.130 --> 00:01:01.130
it seems like this would give you a lot more information with which to predict the price.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:13.130
To introduce a little bit of new notation, we're going to use the variables x subscript 1, x subscript 2, x subscript 3, and x subscript 4 to denote the four features.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:18.130
And for simplicity, let's introduce a little bit more notation.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:26.130
We'll write x subscript j, or sometimes I'll just say for short x sub j to represent the list of features.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:31.130
So here j will go from 1 through 4 because we have four features.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:37.130
I'm going to use lowercase n to denote the total number of features.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.130
So in this example, n is equal to 4.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:48.130
As before, we'll use x superscript i to denote the i-th training example.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:55.130
So here, x superscript i is actually going to be a list of four numbers.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:02:04.130
Or sometimes we'll call this a vector that includes all the features of the i-th training example.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:15.130
So as a concrete example, x superscript in parentheses 2 will be a vector of the features for the second training example.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:20.130
So it will equal to this 1, 4, 1, 6, 3, 2, and 40.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:28.130
And technically, I'm writing these numbers in a row, so sometimes this is called a row vector rather than a column vector.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:31.130
But if you don't know what the difference is, don't worry about it.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:34.130
It's not that important for this purpose.

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:45.130
And to refer to a specific feature in the i-th training example, I will write x superscript i subscript j.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:56.130
So for example, x superscript 2 subscript 3 will be the value of the third feature, that is the number of floors in the second training example.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:00.130
And so that's going to be equal to 2.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:08.130
Sometimes in order to emphasize that this x2 is not a number, but it's actually a list of numbers that is a vector,

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:16.130
we'll draw an arrow on top of that just to visually show that is a vector.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.130
And over here as well.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:22.130
But you don't have to draw this arrow in your notation.

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:31.130
You can think of the arrow as an optional signifier that sometimes use just to emphasize that this is a vector and not a number.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:36.130
Now that we have multiple features, let's take a look at what a model would look like.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:43.130
Previously, this is how we defined the model, where x was a single feature, so a single number.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:48.130
But now with multiple features, we're going to define it differently.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:04:02.130
Instead, the model will be f wb of x equals w1 x1 plus w2 x2 plus w3 x3 plus w4 x4 plus b.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:15.130
Concretely, for housing price prediction, one possible model may be that we estimate the price of the hulls as 0.1 times x1, the size of the hulls,

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:27.130
plus 4 times x2, the number of bedrooms, plus 10 times x3, the number of floors, minus 2 times x4, the age of the hulls in years, plus 80.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:31.130
Let's think a bit about how you might interpret these parameters.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:36.130
If the model is trying to predict the price of the hulls in thousands of dollars,

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:49.130
you can think of this b equals 80 as saying that the base price of a hulls starts off at maybe $80,000, assuming it has no size, no bedrooms, no floor, and no age.

00:04:49.130 --> 00:05:02.130
And you can think of this 0.1 as saying that maybe for every additional square foot, the price will increase by $0.1000 or by $100,

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:12.130
because we're saying that for each square foot, the price increases by 0.1, you know, times $1000, which is $100.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:22.130
And maybe for each additional bathroom, the price increases by $4000, and for each additional floor, the price may increase by $10,000.

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:31.130
And for each additional year of the hulls' age, the price may decrease by $2000 because the parameter is negative 2.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:38.130
And in general, if you have n features, then the model will look like this.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:44.820
Here again is the definition of the model with n features.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:51.820
What we're going to do next is introduce a little bit of notation to rewrite this expression in a simpler but equivalent way.

00:05:52.820 --> 00:06:01.820
Let's define w as a list of numbers that list the parameters w1, w2, w3, all the way through wn.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:04.820
In mathematics, this is called a vector.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:12.820
And sometimes to designate that this is a vector, which just means a list of numbers, I'm going to draw a little arrow on top.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:18.820
You don't always have to draw this arrow, and you can do so or not in your own notation.

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:25.820
So you can think of this little arrow as just an optional signifier to remind us that this is a vector.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:33.820
If you've taken a linear algebra class before, you might recognize that this is a row vector as opposed to a column vector.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:37.820
But if you don't know what those terms mean, you don't need to worry about it.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:42.820
Next, same as before, b is a single number, not a vector.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:50.820
And so this vector w, together with this number b, are the parameters of the model.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:07:03.820
Let me also write x as a list or a vector, again, a row vector, that lists all of the features x1, x2, x3 up through xn.

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:12.820
This is again a vector, so I'm going to add a little arrow up on top to signify.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:28.820
So in the notation up on top, we can also add little arrows here and here to signify that that w and that x are actually these lists of numbers that are actually these vectors.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:49.820
So with this notation, the model can now be rewritten more succinctly as f of x equals the vector w dot, and this dot refers to a dot product from linear algebra, of x the vector plus the number b.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.820
So what does this dot product mean?

00:07:52.820 --> 00:08:21.820
Well, the dot product of two vectors of two lists of numbers, w and x, is computed by taking the corresponding pairs of numbers, w1 and x1, multiplying that, w2, x2, multiplying that, w3, x3, multiplying that, all the way up to wn, xn, multiplying that, and then summing up all of these products.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:45.820
Writing that out, this means that the dot product is equal to w1, x1 plus w2, x2 plus w3, x3 plus all the way up to wn, xn, and then finally we add back in the b on top.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:52.140
And you notice that this gives us exactly the same expression as we had on top.

00:08:53.140 --> 00:09:01.140
So the dot product notation lets you write the model in a more compact form, with fewer characters.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:08.140
The name for this type of linear regression model with multiple input features is multiple linear regression.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:13.140
This is in contrast to univariate regression, which had just one feature.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:23.140
And by the way, you might think this algorithm is called multivariate regression, but that term actually refers to something else that we won't be using here.

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:27.140
So I'm going to refer to this model as multiple linear regression.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:34.140
And so that's it for linear regression with multiple features, which is also called multiple linear regression.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:45.140
In order to implement this, there's a really neat trick called vectorization, which will make it much simpler to implement this and many other learning algorithms.

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:50.140
Let's go on to the next video to take a look at what is vectorization.
